 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Credit card rates are far above the cash rate, and they barely follow it. I couldn't get a personal loan comparison: the RBA's personal loan series came back empty.\n\n**Latest (September 2026, RBA tables F1.1 and F5)**\n\n| Rate | % p.a. | Gap over cash rate |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cash rate target (monthly average) | 4.36 | – |\n| Standard credit card (advertised) | 21.24 | +16.9 points |\n| Low-rate credit card (advertised) | 13.49 | +9.1 points |\n\n**Credit card rates hardly respond to the cash rate**\n- **January 2021 to September 2026:** the cash rate rose 4.26 points (0.10% to 4.36%). Standard card rates rose only 1.30 points (19.94% to 21.24%), and low-rate cards 0.76 points (12.73% to 13.49%).\n- **So the gap has narrowed:** the standard card gap fell from about 19.8 points in January 2021 to 16.9 points now. That's because card rates were already very high when the cash rate was near zero, not because borrowing on a card got cheap.\n- **2022–23 hikes:** the cash rate went from 0.10% to 4.35% between May 2022 and December 2023. Standard card rates didn't move during 2022 (19.94%) and even dipped to 19.82% in February 2023. They only began rising from late 2023.\n- **2025 cuts weren't passed on:** the cash rate fell from 4.35% to 3.60% (February–September 2025), yet standard card rates stayed at 20.99%, after rising to that level in January 2025. Low-rate cards stayed at 13.49%.\n- **2026 hikes:** the cash rate went back up from 3.60% in January to about 4.35% by June. Standard card rates stayed at 20.99% until a rise to 21.24% in September 2026. Low-rate cards were unchanged.\n\n**Personal loans:** the RBA's advertised fixed personal loan rate series came back with no data for this period, and so did its variable personal loan series. It looks discontinued or unavailable, so I can't give a figure.\n\nThese are advertised rates. The RBA notes that rates borrowers actually pay can be lower. Even so, credit card rates are about five times the cash rate. Unlike home loan rates, they're set mostly by lender margins rather than RBA policy, so cash rate cuts usually don't lower what you pay on a card.",
